WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.950 --> 00:00:03.950
Hello, everybody.

00:00:03.950 --> 00:00:05.790
This is Peter Cooper from the NCBI.

00:00:05.790 --> 00:00:10.849
Today's Minute will be on the NCBI Bookshelf:
Textbooks for Free!.

00:00:10.849 --> 00:00:14.530
During the webinar if you have a question,
please type it in the questions pod.

00:00:14.530 --> 00:00:16.210
I will try to answer the questions.

00:00:16.210 --> 00:00:19.340
I am not going to be presenting, Rana Morris
will be presenting.

00:00:19.340 --> 00:00:28.820
At the end we will be able to address some
of them orally and Stacy Lathrop is here from

00:00:28.820 --> 00:00:30.620
Bookshelf to help answer them.

00:00:30.620 --> 00:00:36.330
If there are questions we don't get to, in
fact all questions, will be put into a document

00:00:36.330 --> 00:00:39.910
linked to our webinars and courses page.

00:00:39.910 --> 00:00:46.420
Slides, supplementals and Q&amp;A documents will
be on the FTP site.

00:00:46.420 --> 00:00:52.890
I will turn it over to Rana Morris.

00:00:52.890 --> 00:00:54.270
Morris.

00:00:54.270 --> 00:00:55.270
Thanks for joining us today.

00:00:55.270 --> 00:01:01.890
Today will talk about textbook that are available
on the NCBI bookshelf, how they get there,

00:01:01.890 --> 00:01:06.150
and maybe how you can have your textbook on
the bookshelf as well.

00:01:06.150 --> 00:01:10.780
Since 1999 NCBI has worked with publishers
and authors to provide an additional way for

00:01:10.780 --> 00:01:13.630
readers to access their products.

00:01:13.630 --> 00:01:19.240
The NCBI Bookshelf also called Books for short
houses books reports documents and other literature-based

00:01:19.240 --> 00:01:22.659
media on life sciences and health related
topics.

00:01:22.659 --> 00:01:27.890
The specific topics or scope was selected
by our parent organization, the U.S. National

00:01:27.890 --> 00:01:30.000
Library of Medicine.

00:01:30.000 --> 00:01:35.869
The Bookshelf collects and makes available
only fulltext works not abstracts.

00:01:35.869 --> 00:01:38.679
And they are all in English.

00:01:38.679 --> 00:01:43.950
One of the things that NCBI is known for are
links provided between related pieces of information

00:01:43.950 --> 00:01:47.429
within and between resources throughout NCBI.

00:01:47.429 --> 00:01:51.659
We do this to provide a way for users of our
site to learn and discover more about their

00:01:51.659 --> 00:01:56.569
topics of interest since many of our databases
involve biological data, it was natural to

00:01:56.569 --> 00:02:02.700
provide links between our databases and to
biological and health-related textbooks and

00:02:02.700 --> 00:02:05.849
other books on the Bookshelf.

00:02:05.849 --> 00:02:10.700
These are a large number of databases that
we have in various domain fields.

00:02:10.700 --> 00:02:16.010
We've long been known for our literature databases,
particularly PubMed and PubMed central or

00:02:16.010 --> 00:02:17.319
PMC.

00:02:17.319 --> 00:02:21.640
We've also maintained biological sequence
databases for nucleotides and protein since

00:02:21.640 --> 00:02:27.110
1990 and have added other sequence related
databases as well as those housing data related

00:02:27.110 --> 00:02:31.680
to genes, protein structure and activity,
and chemicals.

00:02:31.680 --> 00:02:36.569
And more recently, have added several health
and clinically focused databases to our suite

00:02:36.569 --> 00:02:41.909
of resources as the years in scientific research
has progressed we are adding more and more.

00:02:41.909 --> 00:02:47.440
The NCBI Bookshelf or Books database is another
literature database that can connect to all

00:02:47.440 --> 00:02:49.049
the resources.

00:02:49.049 --> 00:02:53.349
The link to a lot of the basis science information
is perhaps one reason why textbooks have been

00:02:53.349 --> 00:02:58.819
so successfully added to the NCBI system.

00:02:58.819 --> 00:03:00.980
So why textbooks?

00:03:00.980 --> 00:03:05.159
In fact the NCBI Bookshelf started in 1999
with a single book.

00:03:05.159 --> 00:03:09.580
In addition of one of the most respected molecular
biology books.

00:03:09.580 --> 00:03:12.409
The third edition of molecular biology of
the cell.

00:03:12.409 --> 00:03:18.069
By Alberts, Johnson, Lewis, Raff, Roberts
and Walter.

00:03:18.069 --> 00:03:22.700
The text was submitted to us for inclusion
in the NCBI Bookshelf by Garland publishing

00:03:22.700 --> 00:03:25.540
with the blessing of the authors.

00:03:25.540 --> 00:03:31.719
Recently we caught up with Bruce Alberts who
relayed to us that all of the authors of the

00:03:31.719 --> 00:03:36.209
molecular biology of the cell were pleased
when Garland publishing decided to post the

00:03:36.209 --> 00:03:38.620
third edition of our textbook on Bookshelf.

00:03:38.620 --> 00:03:43.549
And we were especially happy that this free
resource has been heavily used, contributing

00:03:43.549 --> 00:03:50.840
an important U.S. effort to spread science
to students across the globe.

00:03:50.840 --> 00:03:55.030
In addition to molecular biology of the cell,
several all other extremely popular textbooks

00:03:55.030 --> 00:04:00.999
were added early on, including Berb, Tymoczko
and Stryer's biochemistry.

00:04:00.999 --> 00:04:08.140
Barons Medical Microbiology, and Walker, Hall,
and Hurst clinical methods.

00:04:08.140 --> 00:04:12.680
These are considered classic text books which
continued to be well-regarded and are still

00:04:12.680 --> 00:04:13.720
used today.

00:04:13.720 --> 00:04:18.950
A quote from one frequent user of these books,
a professor teaching graduate and professional

00:04:18.950 --> 00:04:24.530
life science and clinical courses, mentioned
that I grew up with Stryer's Biochemistry

00:04:24.530 --> 00:04:27.000
and Alberts molecular biology of the cell.

00:04:27.000 --> 00:04:30.940
All you need to add is Lewin's Genes and I'd
never leave my computer.

00:04:30.940 --> 00:04:36.850
But seriously I've recommended the NCBI Bookshelf
to my students, included links to the textbooks

00:04:36.850 --> 00:04:41.850
in syllabi, and incorporated graphics and
tables with attribution in lectures for over

00:04:41.850 --> 00:04:43.220
12 years now.

00:04:43.220 --> 00:04:47.860
Having access to free, high quality textbooks
is key to being able to meet the needs of

00:04:47.860 --> 00:04:52.540
my graduate, medical and PA students.

00:04:52.540 --> 00:04:57.690
The online version of these texts include
all features of the hard copy books.

00:04:57.690 --> 00:04:59.070
Including graphics, images and tables.

00:04:59.070 --> 00:05:03.530
Which are some of the most heavily viewed
sections of these books.

00:05:03.530 --> 00:05:05.040
But some say these are old.

00:05:05.040 --> 00:05:07.760
Are they still useful or at least are being
used?

00:05:07.760 --> 00:05:10.020
In fact, they are.

00:05:10.020 --> 00:05:15.950
In January 2018, hundreds of thousands of
people were accessing and spending time looking

00:05:15.950 --> 00:05:20.360
through and reading these text books.

00:05:20.360 --> 00:05:25.160
These four are the current most popular and
used entries on the NCBI Bookshelf.

00:05:25.160 --> 00:05:29.960
Okay, so faculty who have grown up with these
classic texts may feel comfortable with them.

00:05:29.960 --> 00:05:34.390
But what about students newly exposed to these
disciplines in their undergraduate studies?

00:05:34.390 --> 00:05:38.090
Why are they still accessing these classic
text.

00:05:38.090 --> 00:05:42.840
Here's a quote from an undergraduate biochemistry
major that is representative of what we heard

00:05:42.840 --> 00:05:44.840
from students.

00:05:44.840 --> 00:05:50.490
Shawn says I can't afford to spend $350-$400
for a single textbook.

00:05:50.490 --> 00:05:54.210
Even if I could, carrying around is a pain
in the neck, literally.

00:05:54.210 --> 00:05:58.440
Being able to look things up on my laptop
or cell phone or anywhere my study group happens

00:05:58.440 --> 00:06:02.020
to be is the only way of got through my biology
courses.

00:06:02.020 --> 00:06:06.090
And unlike what is on most of the web, I know
I can trust what I find at the U.S. National

00:06:06.090 --> 00:06:08.220
Library of Medicine.

00:06:08.220 --> 00:06:12.880
The cost factor as well as the platform for
textbooks are both important factors in student

00:06:12.880 --> 00:06:14.500
usage of textbooks.

00:06:14.500 --> 00:06:20.650
In a blog post on the Wiley network by the
library director of the Western Michigan University

00:06:20.650 --> 00:06:26.850
school of medicine she explored students preferences
and concluded that overall, we know learners

00:06:26.850 --> 00:06:31.250
sometimes prefer print, and other times they
prefer the electronic version but generally,

00:06:31.250 --> 00:06:35.360
surveys on the subject seem to be in agreement
that having access to both an electronic and

00:06:35.360 --> 00:06:38.210
print textbook copy is optimal.

00:06:38.210 --> 00:06:43.160
At the NCBI Bookshelf we have had some new
textbooks added to our inventory in recent

00:06:43.160 --> 00:06:45.160
years.

00:06:45.160 --> 00:06:50.290
And in fact one of them, the essentials of
glycobiology, was unique in that it was conceived

00:06:50.290 --> 00:06:54.980
from the outset by its editors, publisher
and NCBI as a book that would be published

00:06:54.980 --> 00:07:00.300
simultaneously in print by Coldspring Harvard
press and also made available in fulltext

00:07:00.300 --> 00:07:02.530
online at NCBI Bookshelf.

00:07:02.530 --> 00:07:07.340
The editors and publisher hope that this would
serve as a worldwide model for an open education

00:07:07.340 --> 00:07:08.340
vision.

00:07:08.340 --> 00:07:12.430
They recently provided a quote to us.

00:07:12.430 --> 00:07:17.250
We are happy to have collaborated with NCBI
staff whose skills and efficiency ensured

00:07:17.250 --> 00:07:21.650
our book would be discoverable and freely
accessible to researchers, instructors, students,

00:07:21.650 --> 00:07:28.100
and others after simultaneous release in print
and fulltext online, with online-only updates

00:07:28.100 --> 00:07:31.070
available on the NCBI Bookshelf.

00:07:31.070 --> 00:07:35.710
We were very happy to work with Ajit Varki
and John Inglis on this project.

00:07:35.710 --> 00:07:38.750
But why would other publishers want to consider
this type of model?

00:07:38.750 --> 00:07:43.770
Why would publishers be interested in making
textbooks freely available for the customers?

00:07:43.770 --> 00:07:49.621
In a white paper published by Springer Nature
in November 2017 called the OA effect, how

00:07:49.621 --> 00:07:53.360
does open access affect the usage of scholarly
books.

00:07:53.360 --> 00:08:00.230
They revealed that open access, which go even
further than free online textbooks, I\are

00:08:00.230 --> 00:08:04.590
downloaded seven times more than any others
in the first year of publication.

00:08:04.590 --> 00:08:07.860
Are cited 50% more over four years.

00:08:07.860 --> 00:08:12.200
And are mentioned online 10 times more over
three years.

00:08:12.200 --> 00:08:16.480
Essentially, there is a significant amount
of free advertisement and marketing that organically

00:08:16.480 --> 00:08:19.730
grows by making books freely available on
the Internet.

00:08:19.730 --> 00:08:24.570
This dramatically increases the potential
in realized audience for these books.

00:08:24.570 --> 00:08:30.191
In a blog post exploring the data in this
white paper, books and the OA effect, one

00:08:30.191 --> 00:08:35.400
publishers perspective, they mentioned that
in addition to free advertising and expanded

00:08:35.400 --> 00:08:39.680
audience which benefited the publisher, two
other groups seem to strongly support this

00:08:39.680 --> 00:08:44.670
movement for free online open access book
availability.

00:08:44.670 --> 00:08:49.860
Funders or sponsors were motivated by specific
ethical considerations that publicly funded

00:08:49.860 --> 00:08:53.080
research should be open for the public to
read.

00:08:53.080 --> 00:08:57.200
They were also interested in fulfilling their
and their authors expectations for finding

00:08:57.200 --> 00:09:00.840
mechanisms to reach a larger audience.

00:09:00.840 --> 00:09:06.500
Authors motivations included improving access
to research information, enhancing dissemination

00:09:06.500 --> 00:09:11.700
to a wider, broader audience, and also providing
a way for students to be impacted by their

00:09:11.700 --> 00:09:14.310
work at low cost.

00:09:14.310 --> 00:09:18.570
The links to both the white paper and the
blog are up there on the slide.

00:09:18.570 --> 00:09:21.620
At NCBI we can help you do all of this.

00:09:21.620 --> 00:09:22.620
https://oaspa.org/books-oa-effect-one-publishers-perspective/
https://www.springernature.com/gp/open-research/journals-books/books/the-oa-effect

00:09:22.620 --> 00:09:25.340
What the NCBI Bookshelf can do for you.

00:09:25.340 --> 00:09:27.210
So here is what we hope to do.

00:09:27.210 --> 00:09:33.900
Make your textbook findable and usable for
anyone, anywhere, it's on the Internet.

00:09:33.900 --> 00:09:39.070
Optimize indexing of your book in Google and
in PubMed to make it easy to find.

00:09:39.070 --> 00:09:44.360
Enable viewing in any web browser by scrolling
Webpage styles and we also have an e-book

00:09:44.360 --> 00:09:49.550
style PubReader which makes the book easier
to read.

00:09:49.550 --> 00:09:54.000
We also hope to expand your audience to more
than just college classes who have already

00:09:54.000 --> 00:09:56.230
adopted your textbook.

00:09:56.230 --> 00:09:58.630
Because they can access it as well.

00:09:58.630 --> 00:10:03.760
Here is the view of Catherine Jenkins who
is the open access book workflows coordinator

00:10:03.760 --> 00:10:10.530
at the publisher Springer Nature has submitted
many books available on the NCBI Bookshelf.

00:10:10.530 --> 00:10:16.000
The NCBI Bookshelf offers an additional route
for wider discoverability for our open access

00:10:16.000 --> 00:10:22.110
books and Wellcome-funded OA chapters and
helps to direct traffic through these to the

00:10:22.110 --> 00:10:26.029
text hosted on our own platform SpringerLink.

00:10:26.029 --> 00:10:31.270
The HTML display on the device agnostic platform
is user-friendly and renders our books and

00:10:31.270 --> 00:10:37.370
easily navigable sections to create a best
in class online reading experience.

00:10:37.370 --> 00:10:40.730
We value our collaboration.

00:10:40.730 --> 00:10:45.150
So to summarize, NCBI Bookshelf can increase
discoverability of your text and provide a

00:10:45.150 --> 00:10:48.340
link to help readers find your own site and
resources.

00:10:48.340 --> 00:10:52.520
And we are a good collaborative partner who
is able to present your work online for a

00:10:52.520 --> 00:10:53.710
great reader experience.

00:10:53.710 --> 00:10:55.180
Are you interested?

00:10:55.180 --> 00:11:00.220
What do you need to know about getting your
book on the NCBI Bookshelf?

00:11:00.220 --> 00:11:05.030
Please keep in mind the scope of the works
as specified by the U.S. National Library

00:11:05.030 --> 00:11:06.030
of Medicine.

00:11:06.030 --> 00:11:10.600
We take books about life sciences and health
related topics and they must be in English

00:11:10.600 --> 00:11:14.260
and need to be in fulltext, not just abstracts
or citation.

00:11:14.260 --> 00:11:20.130
Who can apply to have their book included
in the NCBI Bookshelf?

00:11:20.130 --> 00:11:25.550
The application to include the book must be
driven by the copyright holder of the text.

00:11:25.550 --> 00:11:29.811
However, that copyright holder needs to make
sure that all participating members of the

00:11:29.811 --> 00:11:36.710
team agree on this submission including authors,
editors, publishers, funders and sponsors.

00:11:36.710 --> 00:11:42.920
So to begin the application process you can
download an application from the NCBI website.

00:11:42.920 --> 00:11:48.779
There's also, for new submitters, a publishers
application to fill out.

00:11:48.779 --> 00:11:53.570
Once those are filled out, you can submit
it to the Bookshelf via email which I will

00:11:53.570 --> 00:11:54.850
provide shortly.

00:11:54.850 --> 00:12:00.080
It is important also to provide details of
the work including the title and an abstract

00:12:00.080 --> 00:12:04.839
for the book as well as bios for the editors
and authors.

00:12:04.839 --> 00:12:07.400
And a copy of the book for review.

00:12:07.400 --> 00:12:12.550
For printed books, there should either be
two hard copies or in an electronic PDF version

00:12:12.550 --> 00:12:13.720
of the book.

00:12:13.720 --> 00:12:20.670
For e-books you can send either the electronic
files or the URL for accessing these files.

00:12:20.670 --> 00:12:23.120
Actually if you just go to the Bookshelf homepage.

00:12:23.120 --> 00:12:28.670
You can find links to helpful information
includig a whole section for people who want

00:12:28.670 --> 00:12:31.320
to have their books on the bookshelf.

00:12:31.320 --> 00:12:34.300
Including a whole set of documents on how
to apply.

00:12:34.300 --> 00:12:39.440
there is information for people who want to
learn how to use the bookshelf.

00:12:39.440 --> 00:12:41.270
Including a quick start guide.

00:12:41.270 --> 00:12:45.290
And FAQs that answer common questions that
people want to know.

00:12:45.290 --> 00:12:47.020
Can I use figures from the book?

00:12:47.020 --> 00:12:52.030
How do I cite books from the Bookshelf ? Can
a publisher also provide their work on other

00:12:52.030 --> 00:12:53.880
sites in addition to the bookshelf?

00:12:53.880 --> 00:12:57.060
By the way, the answer is yes.

00:12:57.060 --> 00:13:02.120
So in conclusion we've got a couple of direct
URLs for you.

00:13:02.120 --> 00:13:10.089
The NCBI's Bookshelf homepage you just saw,
as well as links to other helpful documentation.

00:13:10.089 --> 00:13:15.130
These are linked directly on the homepage
from the Bookshelf.

00:13:15.130 --> 00:13:20.660
Here is the direct link for the information
for authors and publishers and how to apply.

00:13:20.660 --> 00:13:23.350
https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/about/publishers/
If you have additional questions or inquiries

00:13:23.350 --> 00:13:30.830
you can reach the NCBI Bookshelf staff directly
at this email, bookshelf@ncbi.nlm.nih.gov

00:13:30.830 --> 00:13:38.350
We would like to finish with a place where
you can go to learn more about any of our

00:13:38.350 --> 00:13:41.589
resources.

00:13:41.589 --> 00:13:44.390
The NCBI Insights blog.

00:13:44.390 --> 00:13:46.720
The NCBI YouTube channel.

00:13:46.720 --> 00:13:52.029
And for any questions , general questions
about NCBI, you can always send it into info

00:13:52.029 --> 00:13:56.880
at NCBI.NLM.NIH.GOV address.

00:13:56.880 --> 00:14:00.070
Even for the Bookshelf you can send it there
and we can route it over to the Bookshelf

00:14:00.070 --> 00:14:01.080
people as well.

00:14:01.080 --> 00:14:03.660
Thank you for your attention.

00:14:03.660 --> 00:14:08.060
We now have some time for some questions that
you have written in the chat pod.

00:14:08.060 --> 00:14:12.210
And the head of the NCBI Bookshelf group,
Stacy Lathrop, is here and she can answer

00:14:12.210 --> 00:14:14.610
your questions.

00:14:14.610 --> 00:14:16.279
This is Peter Cooper again.

00:14:16.279 --> 00:14:18.740
We have one question so far.

00:14:18.740 --> 00:14:20.500
This is directed to Stacy.

00:14:20.500 --> 00:14:25.040
Would you be interested in a previous edition
of a text if a new addition has already been

00:14:25.040 --> 00:14:26.830
published?

00:14:26.830 --> 00:14:28.240
Thank you for your question.

00:14:28.240 --> 00:14:31.980
We do accept previous editions.

00:14:31.980 --> 00:14:35.190
We do prefer the most recent addition.

00:14:35.190 --> 00:14:40.600
We have some older editions so if it's not
the newest edition, we will take the next

00:14:40.600 --> 00:14:44.230
or second to most recent one.

00:14:44.230 --> 00:14:45.230
Thanks.

00:14:45.230 --> 00:14:47.660
Here's a question.

00:14:47.660 --> 00:14:51.870
How many books are on the NCBI Bookshelf?

00:14:51.870 --> 00:14:56.190
We now have over 6000 books in Bookshelf.

00:14:56.190 --> 00:15:03.410
I do not have the exact number but I can provide
that, and I could do a search.

00:15:03.410 --> 00:15:09.279
So the easiest way to see how many books we
have on Bookshelf would be to go to our browse

00:15:09.279 --> 00:15:10.970
titles page.

00:15:10.970 --> 00:15:14.240
I am clicking on it here.

00:15:14.240 --> 00:15:23.089
Some of our books appear in our archives so
they are not in our default browse or search.

00:15:23.089 --> 00:15:28.640
This is usually for clinical content because
we do also have a lot of systematic reviews

00:15:28.640 --> 00:15:33.770
and clinical guidelines where the currency
is important for our agreements we do not

00:15:33.770 --> 00:15:35.959
make these available by default.

00:15:35.959 --> 00:15:40.150
So the complete number of titles we have,
6042 titles.

00:15:40.150 --> 00:15:49.640
And we are pretty much adding between five
and 20 per week.

00:15:49.640 --> 00:15:51.220
Thanks, Stacy.

00:15:51.220 --> 00:15:52.779
Here's another question.

00:15:52.779 --> 00:15:56.540
Is there an iPad, iPhone app for the Bookshelf?

00:15:56.540 --> 00:16:01.540
We do not currently have an iPad or iPhone
app.

00:16:01.540 --> 00:16:09.320
What we do have is what we call a PubReader
version which I can actually show you here.

00:16:09.320 --> 00:16:11.339
This is actually a report rather than a textbook.

00:16:11.339 --> 00:16:14.350
But it will show you what we have.

00:16:14.350 --> 00:16:22.029
If you click on views to pub reader, it's
an HTML 5 page.

00:16:22.029 --> 00:16:28.700
You can do a find within the book but you
can't search across the resource.

00:16:28.700 --> 00:16:36.370
The view looks different on a PC, it is kind
of device-smart.

00:16:36.370 --> 00:16:45.830
So if you are on an iPad, it will look pretty
similar to this.

00:16:45.830 --> 00:16:50.500
And then on the iPhone it doesn't scroll by
page it will just scroll down the page.

00:16:50.500 --> 00:16:53.320
It's a little bit different on the iPhone.

00:16:53.320 --> 00:16:56.100
Which I can't show you, unfortunately.

00:16:56.100 --> 00:16:57.160
Okay.

00:16:57.160 --> 00:16:58.230
Thanks.

00:16:58.230 --> 00:17:01.540
This will be the last question.

